WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.157 --> 00:00:01.990
- One.

00:00:02.866 --> 00:00:03.873
Two. (clapping)

00:00:03.873 --> 00:00:04.706
Three.

00:00:05.091 --> 00:00:05.924
Four.

00:00:05.984 --> 00:00:07.480
- [Woman] Good job.
- Five.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.313
- Six.

00:00:08.537 --> 00:00:09.816
My name is Eilat Nahum,

00:00:09.816 --> 00:00:12.700
I'm owner and administrator
of Beit Shalom.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.591
Beit Shalom basically mean house of peace,

00:00:15.591 --> 00:00:20.341
and it's RCFE, residential
care facility for the elderly.

00:00:21.765 --> 00:00:24.695
- You see people that have
been professors at UCLA,

00:00:24.695 --> 00:00:27.259
doctors, pilots, and all of a sudden,

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:29.697
they can't walk, they forget their name,

00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:31.852
it's hard to watch and to know that

00:00:31.852 --> 00:00:34.538
it's just a matter of
time before I get there,

00:00:34.538 --> 00:00:35.918
before my mother gets there,

00:00:35.918 --> 00:00:37.346
before my grandmother gets there.

00:00:37.346 --> 00:00:39.797
It's been a few years of
a lot of reality checks

00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:41.214
on a daily basis.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.158
So this second facility
is just down the block

00:00:47.158 --> 00:00:48.908
from our first place.

00:00:49.454 --> 00:00:51.972
- This house has been
a big blessing for us,

00:00:51.972 --> 00:00:54.222
my mother-in-law got very, very sick,

00:00:54.222 --> 00:00:56.075
and I wanted to bring her
here, so in order for me

00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:57.989
to bring her here, I
had to have a facility

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:00.341
with the right staff,
with the right caregivers,

00:01:00.341 --> 00:01:01.898
and I got my license.

00:01:01.898 --> 00:01:04.022
Two weeks later, she passed away.

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:07.114
So this is like the
memory of my mother-in-law

00:01:07.114 --> 00:01:09.954
and also it's been a big
blessing for all of us.

00:01:09.954 --> 00:01:11.454
How are you, Mama?

00:01:11.859 --> 00:01:13.359
How is everything?

00:01:13.462 --> 00:01:15.379
- Most things are okay.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.199
- Good, good, I'm happy.

00:01:17.199 --> 00:01:19.180
- So basically, we have 24 clients,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.365
one of them is Elsie,
she does beads, flowers,

00:01:22.365 --> 00:01:23.865
and beautiful art.

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:27.516
- This was my hobby of
making beaded flowers.

00:01:27.516 --> 00:01:31.018
- How long does it take
you to make one of these?

00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:32.101
- Oh, well...

00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:33.236
Some time.

00:01:33.695 --> 00:01:36.844
- Taking care of the
elderly is very rewarding,

00:01:36.844 --> 00:01:39.261
especially when they improve.

00:01:39.685 --> 00:01:43.438
Some of them come here
and they are quite frail

00:01:43.438 --> 00:01:46.290
and I see how they
progress and they actually

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.207
become healthier, participate for--

00:01:50.632 --> 00:01:53.773
- How are you? (kisses)

00:01:53.773 --> 00:01:55.148
How is everything?
- Good.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.766
- We also have another
client, her name is Loella.

00:01:57.766 --> 00:01:59.874
When she first moved into
our place, she was not able

00:01:59.874 --> 00:02:02.024
to walk, or talk, or eat even.

00:02:02.024 --> 00:02:05.577
Now, thank God she's eating,
walking, and talking.

00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:07.160
And she's so happy.

00:02:08.884 --> 00:02:11.051
- What letter is this one?

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:13.288
- What letter is that?
- [Man] H.

00:02:13.288 --> 00:02:14.121
- That's right.

00:02:14.121 --> 00:02:15.786
And this one is?
- I.

00:02:15.786 --> 00:02:18.344
- In the afternoon, basically
we do bingo, puzzles,

00:02:18.344 --> 00:02:20.660
arts and craft, because
it's very good for their

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:21.579
cognitive skills.

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:24.351
- Got a PhD and taught at Washington State

00:02:24.351 --> 00:02:27.678
and then UCLA, retired
professor from there.

00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:30.167
If you're not used to living
in an institutional care,

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:33.412
it's adjustment, it would
be good to have more younger

00:02:33.412 --> 00:02:36.336
people have some experience
with facilities like this.

00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:38.675
- Can you put H back? Where's H?

00:02:38.675 --> 00:02:41.238
No, no, no, don't eat it. (laughs)

00:02:41.238 --> 00:02:45.343
- The challenge in this job,
it is when people decline.

00:02:45.343 --> 00:02:48.425
And they actually see
how they deteriorate,

00:02:48.425 --> 00:02:51.758
you find yourself being really helpless.

00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:57.071
- Let's get them out and
then dinner going also.

00:02:57.071 --> 00:02:59.710
Today, they have a special treat.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:03.602
You gonna have dinner?
(speaks foreign language)

00:03:03.602 --> 00:03:04.435
Dinner!

00:03:10.809 --> 00:03:13.886
- I feel the elderly are neglected a lot

00:03:13.886 --> 00:03:17.175
by their own families,
and it's sad because

00:03:17.175 --> 00:03:21.592
elderly kind of want to have
their own family around.

00:03:22.904 --> 00:03:25.182
- I have some family members
who don't even visit,

00:03:25.182 --> 00:03:27.788
and that's the sad part and
sometime we try to call them,

00:03:27.788 --> 00:03:30.268
to speak to them, they
don't even answer the phone.

00:03:30.268 --> 00:03:32.515
- I haven't been happy since my wife died,

00:03:32.515 --> 00:03:36.777
my daughter died, and my son
ran off with all the money.

00:03:36.777 --> 00:03:39.694
So, ain't been too many happy days.

00:03:40.422 --> 00:03:43.712
- That's one of the things,
it really hurt me deep inside.

00:03:43.712 --> 00:03:46.271
Just take the moment and
come and visit your parents.

00:03:46.271 --> 00:03:47.554
If it was the other way around,

00:03:47.554 --> 00:03:49.117
your parents would come and visit you.

00:03:49.117 --> 00:03:51.469
One parent can take care of 10 kids,

00:03:51.469 --> 00:03:53.969
10 kids cannot take care of one parent.

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:55.911
- When I was living on my own,

00:03:55.911 --> 00:03:58.293
I used to celebrate the holidays,

00:03:58.293 --> 00:04:02.440
and I would always call up
nursing homes and ask them

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.647
to please send me a few of their people,

00:04:05.647 --> 00:04:08.527
so that their people could
then enjoy the holidays

00:04:08.527 --> 00:04:11.906
to their fullest and not
spend it lonely in a room

00:04:11.906 --> 00:04:12.882
by themselves.

00:04:12.882 --> 00:04:16.778
Please remember the elders,
because they have contributed

00:04:16.778 --> 00:04:19.111
so much to all of our lives.

00:04:19.588 --> 00:04:21.175
- I had a client and I'd ask him,

00:04:21.175 --> 00:04:22.842
"Sheldon, is there anything that you need,

00:04:22.842 --> 00:04:24.709
"do you want me to do anything for you,

00:04:24.709 --> 00:04:26.418
"is there any food you want
me to pick up for you,"

00:04:26.418 --> 00:04:27.346
and he said,

00:04:27.346 --> 00:04:31.763
"No, just please, if you
could come and talk to me. "

00:04:31.952 --> 00:04:33.386
- One, two, three, four--

00:04:33.386 --> 00:04:37.219
("This Little Light of Mine")

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.979
- Just visit your
grandparents, visit your aunt

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:44.779
who's 90 years old, who's home
by herself with a caregiver,

00:04:44.779 --> 00:04:47.987
and have a conversation with
them, spend 10-15 minutes

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:51.987
with them and you will
see how much they thrive.

00:04:52.688 --> 00:04:55.767
♪ I'm gonna let it shine ♪

00:04:55.767 --> 00:05:00.600
♪ Everywhere I go, I'm
gonna let it shine ♪

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.714
- There is a beautiful, beautiful psalm

00:05:05.714 --> 00:05:08.221
in the book of Psalms,
which King David wrote,

00:05:08.221 --> 00:05:09.054
that says, in Hebrew,

00:05:09.054 --> 00:05:11.936
(speaks in foreign language)

00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:13.848
which translates to, "Don't cast me away,

00:05:13.848 --> 00:05:18.431
at old age, and when I lose
my strength and my ability,

00:05:18.639 --> 00:05:20.056
don't let me go."

00:05:20.204 --> 00:05:23.212
And I live by that sentence,
it's meant for our parents

00:05:23.212 --> 00:05:25.527
and for our loved ones, when they get old,

00:05:25.527 --> 00:05:28.168
to not throw them away,
to not cast them away,

00:05:28.168 --> 00:05:31.261
to not let go, but to be there for them,

00:05:31.261 --> 00:05:33.181
especially in the last
years of their lives,

00:05:33.181 --> 00:05:36.348
to nurture them, to take care of them,

00:05:36.474 --> 00:05:41.224
and when it's your time, people
will do the same for you.

